@implements IDisposable
@inject IAppUpdateService Updates
@inject IServiceProvider Services

@*
    The app-shell footer, mounted once by MainLayout and therefore outside every
    routed page. It carries the two controls that are not about the page in front
    of you — which build this window is running, and how to say that something in
    it is wrong — and, between them, the one thing on the band that changes while
    you look at it: whether the window is saving.

    Both used to live in Home's header, in among the repository scope, the view
    switcher and the pane strip — a region that is entirely about the work on
    screen. That arrangement made two window-wide concerns into a page's
    property, and the cost showed up twice. Settings had to mount
    FeedbackReportButton a second time to have a Report issue button at all, and
    the version was simply not reachable from Settings, which is the screen a
    reader is most likely to be on when they want to know what they are running.
    Neither is a page's fact, so neither belongs to a page: the layout mounts
    this once, and a third route gets both without saying anything.

    Nothing interactive here is hand-rolled. The version is AppButton wearing
    `.header-util` — the utility-button shape, which is what that class has
    always described rather than "a button in the header" — and the update window
    is the shared Modal. This component is only the composition, and the
    composition is why it lives in Backlog.Desktop.UI rather than in the shared
    library: it needs IAppUpdateService and DevelopmentWorkspaceLabel, and
    UiLibraryBoundaryTests holds the library to referencing no application code.
*@

<footer class="app-footer" data-testid="app-footer">
    @* The footer keeps the current version visible. Checking and installing
       updates happen in the separate update window opened from this control,
       which is what keeps a band this thin down to one line of text.

       When the host runs out of a checkout it registers which one, and that
       takes the version's place rather than sitting beside it: several worktrees
       are run side by side and which window belongs to which is the thing you
       cannot tell by looking, so it gets the whole control. The version is still
       one click away, in the update window this opens. The harness footers show
       both at once because they have a whole band for fine print and no header
       to fall back on; this one has two live controls to fit instead. *@
    <AppButton BaseClass="header-util app-version"
               Variant="ButtonVariant.None"
               TestId="app-version"
               title="Open update window"
               aria-label="@VersionControlLabel"
               OnClick="OpenUpdateWindow">
        @if (_workspace is null)
        {
            <text>Version <code>@Updates.CurrentVersion</code></text>
        }
        else
        {
            <text>Worktree <code data-testid="app-workspace">@_workspace.Marker</code></text>
        }
    </AppButton>

    @* The save state, beside the version rather than out at the right edge,
       because the auto margin lives here now. That is the whole reason this
       wrapper exists: with the margin on the version instead, the indicator
       appearing would push Report issue left and its settling back to quiet would
       push it right again, three times a save, under the reader's cursor. The slot
       eats that movement as slack in the middle.

       The wrapper is unconditional and the thing inside it is not. SaveIndicator
       renders nothing at Idle, which is how a band that is now on every route stays
       quiet on the routes that are not editing anything — see
       .design/interaction-guidelines.md#save-state-indicator-vocabulary, where
       "Saved" is explicitly not allowed to nag. *@
    <span class="app-footer__status">
        <SaveIndicator State="@SaveIndicatorState"
                       SavingText="Saving…"
                       SavedText="Saved"
                       FailedText="Couldn't save"
                       id="save-state-indicator"
                       TestId="save-state-indicator" />
    </span>

    @* Its own component, and it stays that way: the trigger and its dialog are
       one state machine, and it is the component that owns it. What changed is
       how many places mount it — one, here — not what it is. *@
    <FeedbackReportButton />
</footer>

@* The update window is a sibling of the band rather than a child of it, exactly
   as it was a sibling of the header before. Modal renders `position: fixed`, so
   it is out of flow either way, but a fixed descendant of a 40-pixel flex row is
   one stray `transform` on that row away from being clipped to it — see the note
   on `.app-footer` in app.css.

   It stays in this component regardless, because the trigger and the dialog are
   one state machine and splitting them across two components would only mean
   passing the state between them. Keeping them together is also what a focus
   restore needs: .design/accessibility.md requires closing a dialog to return
   focus to the control that opened it, and a restore has nowhere to return to
   unless the trigger is still rendered.

   That restore does not happen yet, and not because of anything here. Modal
   moves focus into itself when it opens and has no restore path at all, so
   Escape leaves focus on the document body — measured, and true of every Modal
   in the app, this one no more than the rest. Fixing it belongs in Modal, where
   every dialog gets it at once. What is new is only that the control focus
   should come back to is now at the bottom of the document rather than the top,
   which makes the missing restore cost a reader more than it used to. *@
<Modal Open="_appUpdateWindowOpen"
       OpenChanged="value => { if (!value) CloseUpdateWindow(); }"
       Element="section"
       BaseClass="app-update-dialog"
       BackdropCssClass="app-update-backdrop"
       BackdropRole="presentation"
       UseBodyWrapper="false"
       CloseOnBackdropClick="false"
       CloseOnEscape="@(!_updateBusy)"
       TestId="app-update-dialog"
       aria-labelledby="app-update-title"
       aria-describedby="app-update-description">
    <Header>
        <header class="app-update-dialog__header">
            <div>
                <p class="app-update-dialog__eyebrow">Application updates</p>
                <h2 id="app-update-title">Update Backlog</h2>
            </div>
            <IconButton AriaLabel="Close update window" CssClass="btn--ghost" Disabled="@_updateBusy" OnClick="CloseUpdateWindow">x</IconButton>
        </header>
    </Header>
    <ChildContent>
        <p id="app-update-description" class="app-update-dialog__description">
            You are running Version <code>@Updates.CurrentVersion</code>. Check for a newer build here without crowding the footer.
        </p>
        <div class="app-update-dialog__actions">
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Secondary"
                       data-testid="check-for-updates"
                       Disabled="@_updateBusy"
                       OnClick="CheckForUpdatesAsync">
                @AppUpdatePresentation.CheckLabel(_checkingForAppUpdate)
            </AppButton>
            @if (_appUpdateReady)
            {
                <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Primary"
                           data-testid="install-update"
                           Disabled="@_updateBusy"
                           OnClick="InstallUpdateAsync">
                    @(_installingAppUpdate ? "Installing..." : "Install update")
                </AppButton>
            }
        </div>
        <Alert Message="@_appUpdateMessage" BaseClass="@_appUpdateStatusClass" TestId="app-update-status" Role="status" />
    </ChildContent>
</Modal>

@code {
    // Null in an installed build, where no host registers one and the footer
    // shows the version instead.
    private DevelopmentWorkspaceLabel? _workspace;

    // Whatever this window is currently saving, or null in a host that registers
    // nothing that saves. The footer never learns who is behind it.
    private ISaveStatusSource? _saveStatus;

    private string? _appUpdateMessage;
    private string _appUpdateStatusClass = AppUpdatePresentation.StatusClass(AppUpdateAvailability.Unknown);
    private bool _checkingForAppUpdate;
    private bool _installingAppUpdate;
    private bool _appUpdateReady;
    private bool _appUpdateWindowOpen;

    private bool _updateBusy => _checkingForAppUpdate || _installingAppUpdate;

    private string VersionControlLabel => _workspace is null
        ? AppUpdatePresentation.VersionWindowLabel(Updates.CurrentVersion)
        : AppUpdatePresentation.WorkspaceWindowLabel(_workspace.Marker);

    private SaveState SaveIndicatorState => _saveStatus?.Current ?? SaveState.Idle;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // Asked for rather than injected, so a host that registers nothing —
        // every installed build — still resolves this component. An @inject would
        // fail to construct the layout's footer, which is now every page.
        _workspace = Services.GetService<DevelopmentWorkspaceLabel>();

        // Same reason, one line down. A settings-only or diagnostics head has
        // nothing that saves, and the band it gets should be the band without an
        // indicator rather than no window at all.
        _saveStatus = Services.GetService<ISaveStatusSource>();
        if (_saveStatus is not null) _saveStatus.Changed += OnSaveStatusChanged;
    }

    // The hop onto the renderer is mandatory rather than defensive: Changed is
    // raised from Task continuations — the backlog's debounce flush and the timer
    // that puts "Saved" away again — and neither of those is on this circuit's
    // thread. Same idiom as TasksPane.HandleStateChanged.
    private void OnSaveStatusChanged() => _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public void Dispose()
    {
        if (_saveStatus is not null) _saveStatus.Changed -= OnSaveStatusChanged;
    }

    private void OpenUpdateWindow() => _appUpdateWindowOpen = true;

    private void CloseUpdateWindow()
    {
        if (_updateBusy) return;

        _appUpdateWindowOpen = false;
    }

    private async Task CheckForUpdatesAsync()
    {
        if (_updateBusy) return;

        _checkingForAppUpdate = true;
        _appUpdateMessage = null;
        _appUpdateReady = false;

        try
        {
            var result = await Updates.CheckForUpdateAsync();
            _appUpdateReady = result.UpdateReady;
            _appUpdateMessage = result.Message;
            _appUpdateStatusClass = AppUpdatePresentation.StatusClass(result.Availability);
        }
        catch
        {
            _appUpdateReady = false;
            _appUpdateMessage = "The update check could not be completed.";
            _appUpdateStatusClass = AppUpdatePresentation.StatusClass(AppUpdateAvailability.Failed);
        }
        finally
        {
            _checkingForAppUpdate = false;
        }
    }

    private async Task InstallUpdateAsync()
    {
        if (_updateBusy) return;

        _installingAppUpdate = true;

        try
        {
            var result = await Updates.StartUpdateAsync();
            _appUpdateMessage = result.Message;
            _appUpdateStatusClass = AppUpdatePresentation.StatusClass(
                result.Started ? AppUpdateAvailability.UpToDate : AppUpdateAvailability.Failed);
            if (!result.Started) _appUpdateReady = false;
        }
        catch
        {
            _appUpdateMessage = "The update could not be installed.";
            _appUpdateStatusClass = AppUpdatePresentation.StatusClass(AppUpdateAvailability.Failed);
        }
        finally
        {
            _installingAppUpdate = false;
        }
    }
}
